00:00:50.316 --> 00:00:51.284
Oh, OK.

00:00:51.284 --> 00:00:53.353
We were here.

00:00:53.353 --> 00:00:58.458
So on Wednesday-- and before
we fill this chart out,

00:00:58.458 --> 00:01:01.494
I want to go back and make
sure we, kind of, get this.

00:01:01.494 --> 00:01:02.562
Right?

00:01:02.562 --> 00:01:04.931
We talked about the
Bragg condition.

00:01:04.931 --> 00:01:12.505
And we talked about how the
unique thing about x-rays

00:01:12.505 --> 00:01:17.544
is that their wavelengths
are right in that,

00:01:17.544 --> 00:01:21.648
sort of, precious distance
regime of the spacing of atoms

00:01:21.648 --> 00:01:23.015
in a crystal.

00:01:23.015 --> 00:01:27.253
And so if I shine
them on the crystal,

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:29.389
then they can diffract.

00:01:29.389 --> 00:01:33.726
And they can have constructive
and destructive interference,

00:01:33.726 --> 00:01:37.130
but only if they line up.

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:39.699
And that's what the Bragg
diffraction tells us.

00:01:39.699 --> 00:01:42.801
It tells us the
the Bragg condition

00:01:42.801 --> 00:01:43.937
for when they line up.

00:01:43.937 --> 00:01:48.074
And in particular, this
is a pretty nice image.

00:01:48.074 --> 00:01:53.446
So you can see here's
one x-ray coming in.

00:01:53.446 --> 00:01:54.481
And it's a wave.

00:01:54.481 --> 00:01:55.381
It's a wave.

00:01:55.381 --> 00:01:56.850
So it's going to be a
wave that goes like that,

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:58.518
and then it bounces
off of this surface.

00:01:58.518 --> 00:02:01.488
Now a second one doesn't
bounce off that top surface.

00:02:01.488 --> 00:02:04.724
It bounces off of
the one beneath it.

00:02:04.724 --> 00:02:05.225
OK?

00:02:05.225 --> 00:02:07.393
And they're separated
by a distance D.

00:02:07.393 --> 00:02:10.196
So this could be
any set of planes.

00:02:10.196 --> 00:02:11.598
Right?

00:02:11.598 --> 00:02:13.133
So here are my planes now.

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:16.302
So these black
atoms are one plane.

00:02:16.302 --> 00:02:16.870
Right?

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:19.239
But these yellow
ones are another one.

00:02:19.239 --> 00:02:21.808
That's another plane
that you could see.

00:02:21.808 --> 00:02:24.377
Remember, one of the assumptions
of the Bragg condition

00:02:24.377 --> 00:02:26.579
is that these planes
are like mirrors.

00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:29.415
So we lose a sense of where
the atoms are exactly,

00:02:29.415 --> 00:02:31.017
and we just write
a mirror there.

00:02:31.017 --> 00:02:32.752
And we say it's
reflecting off the mirror.

00:02:32.752 --> 00:02:33.753
So where's the next one?

00:02:33.753 --> 00:02:36.022
And you can take
the orange ones.

00:02:36.022 --> 00:02:37.023
That's another plane.

00:02:37.023 --> 00:02:40.360
The red ones and so forth.

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:43.129
Now you have a wave on top.

00:02:43.129 --> 00:02:43.930
OK?

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:45.098
You've got a wave on top.

00:02:45.098 --> 00:02:47.634
And then, you got a wave coming
down and bouncing off the one

00:02:47.634 --> 00:02:51.971
beneath it and for those
to constructively interfere

00:02:51.971 --> 00:02:55.441
so that you pick up a
signal in the detector.

00:02:55.441 --> 00:02:57.543
There's only one way
for that to happen.

00:02:57.543 --> 00:03:00.513
And that's if this
distance plus that distance

00:03:00.513 --> 00:03:03.283
is equal to a multiple
of the wavelength.

00:03:03.283 --> 00:03:03.783
Right?

00:03:03.783 --> 00:03:08.121
And that is the Bragg condition.

00:03:08.121 --> 00:03:10.623
It has to come all the way
down to here, which makes

00:03:10.623 --> 00:03:12.759
that theta equal to that theta.

00:03:12.759 --> 00:03:13.293
You see that?

00:03:13.293 --> 00:03:16.796
Because they've gone up to
this point, up to that line.

00:03:16.796 --> 00:03:18.264
They've gone the same distance.

00:03:18.264 --> 00:03:20.466
So they're waving together.

00:03:20.466 --> 00:03:21.034
All right?

00:03:21.034 --> 00:03:23.536
And it's that point
where now this

00:03:23.536 --> 00:03:26.339
is the extra that the
one that bounces off

00:03:26.339 --> 00:03:30.176
of this plane, that's the
extra distance it goes.

00:03:30.176 --> 00:03:31.778
That's the Bragg condition.

00:03:31.778 --> 00:03:32.545
OK?

00:03:32.545 --> 00:03:33.680
That's the Bragg condition.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:36.683
And remember, in this class,
OK, so it could be any multiple.

00:03:36.683 --> 00:03:39.819
But here, we're just
going to assume n equals 1

00:03:39.819 --> 00:03:42.121
because it's the easiest case.

00:03:42.121 --> 00:03:43.089
OK, good.

00:03:43.089 --> 00:03:45.625
That's the Bragg condition.

00:03:45.625 --> 00:03:50.096
Then, we did
something beyond just

00:03:50.096 --> 00:03:54.767
that because that tells
us what the wavelength has

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:57.870
to be to get a spot,
to get something

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:02.041
the detector can see because
it constructively interferes.

00:04:02.041 --> 00:04:03.610
Everything else is destructive.

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:05.345
No signal.

00:04:05.345 --> 00:04:07.380
But we did one more thing.

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:12.285
We also said that d
for some set of planes.

00:04:12.285 --> 00:04:15.388
Remember, this HKL is
just telling me which

00:04:15.388 --> 00:04:17.123
one of these I'm talking about.

00:04:17.123 --> 00:04:18.257
That's what HKL is.

00:04:18.257 --> 00:04:20.493
I'm going to pick
one of these planes.

00:04:20.493 --> 00:04:23.129
And the distance
between them is DHKL.

00:04:23.129 --> 00:04:24.731
OK, good.

00:04:24.731 --> 00:04:29.569
But we know from before,
from our Miller times,

00:04:29.569 --> 00:04:33.172
we know that that is equal to
the square root of a squared

00:04:33.172 --> 00:04:36.709
plus k squared plus l
squared for the [INAUDIBLE]

00:04:36.709 --> 00:04:37.844
we're talking about.

00:04:37.844 --> 00:04:38.544
All right?

00:04:38.544 --> 00:04:41.948
So you see, it's kind
of Bragg plus Miller.

00:04:41.948 --> 00:04:47.453
Bragg plus distance Miller.

00:04:47.453 --> 00:04:48.021
Right?

00:04:48.021 --> 00:04:49.389
Miller cubic.

00:04:49.389 --> 00:04:52.058
That's what we're
really combining here.

00:04:52.058 --> 00:04:54.394
The distance given
by this formula

00:04:54.394 --> 00:04:57.930
when we talk about Miller
planes, that combined

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:00.233
gave us this.

00:05:00.233 --> 00:05:07.040
Lambda over 2a
squared equals sine

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:15.014
squared theta HKL over h squared
plus k squared plus elsewhere.

00:05:15.014 --> 00:05:17.850
And all I've done is
substitute and square.

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:19.118
OK?

00:05:19.118 --> 00:05:21.454
And we did one more
thing on Wednesday.

00:05:21.454 --> 00:05:22.722
And we talked about constance.

00:05:22.722 --> 00:05:25.325
And we said, well, if something
is equal to a constant,

00:05:25.325 --> 00:05:27.694
then it's a constant.

00:05:27.694 --> 00:05:28.328
All right?

00:05:28.328 --> 00:05:30.063
That's some good math.

00:05:30.063 --> 00:05:31.030
That's some good math.

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:32.932
And so we said, well,
that's the whole beauty

00:05:32.932 --> 00:05:35.368
of these characteristic
x-rays because if you

00:05:35.368 --> 00:05:42.642
take a k alpha line from copper,
it's always 1.54 angstroms.

00:05:42.642 --> 00:05:46.045
It's always lambda equals 1.54.

00:05:46.045 --> 00:05:47.347
That's what's so nice about it.

00:05:47.347 --> 00:05:48.681
Doesn't depend on anything else.

00:05:48.681 --> 00:05:51.818
Just the metal you put
in there is the target.

00:05:51.818 --> 00:05:53.853
It's called a target
because you're

00:05:53.853 --> 00:05:55.722
targeting it with electrons.

00:05:55.722 --> 00:05:59.225
So the copper target, k
alpha, lambda constant.

00:05:59.225 --> 00:06:00.393
Lattice constant?

00:06:00.393 --> 00:06:02.662
That's built into the name.

00:06:02.662 --> 00:06:04.163
Constant.

00:06:04.163 --> 00:06:09.602
Constant equals the angle and
the plane it's reflecting off.

00:06:09.602 --> 00:06:13.806
And so our job then in
crystallography and doing

00:06:13.806 --> 00:06:17.443
x-ray diffraction, our
job is to figure out how

00:06:17.443 --> 00:06:19.679
to keep this thing a constant.

00:06:19.679 --> 00:06:21.347
The same constant.

00:06:21.347 --> 00:06:21.848
All right?

00:06:21.848 --> 00:06:24.450
That's what constant means.

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:25.284
OK?

00:06:25.284 --> 00:06:25.985
That's our job.

00:06:25.985 --> 00:06:27.286
And that's where we left off.

00:06:27.286 --> 00:06:29.889
And we say, OK, well let's
take this to the next level.

00:06:29.889 --> 00:06:31.190
Let's do an actual experiment.

00:06:31.190 --> 00:06:33.393
Now how do you do experiments?

00:06:33.393 --> 00:06:35.094
Well you have equipment.

00:06:35.094 --> 00:06:37.730
And this is what a really
cool XRD machine looks like.

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:40.400
This is what happens when
you don't have any money.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:41.467
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:41.467 --> 00:06:43.403
So this is what's in my lab.

00:06:43.403 --> 00:06:44.237
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:44.237 --> 00:06:45.638
It's true.

00:06:45.638 --> 00:06:46.706
You can come see it.

00:06:46.706 --> 00:06:49.842
But you see, the point is
they knew the same thing.

00:06:49.842 --> 00:06:52.011
We don't need fancy
equipment at MIT.

00:06:52.011 --> 00:06:54.547
No, it's the ideas
that matter that you're

00:06:54.547 --> 00:06:55.982
putting into this thing.

00:06:55.982 --> 00:06:58.317
But that's kind of cool still.

00:06:58.317 --> 00:07:00.086
I like the way that looks.

00:07:00.086 --> 00:07:00.753
Same thing.

00:07:00.753 --> 00:07:01.254
Right?

00:07:01.254 --> 00:07:02.188
Source.

00:07:02.188 --> 00:07:03.289
Sample.

00:07:03.289 --> 00:07:04.323
Detector.

00:07:04.323 --> 00:07:05.124
That's hard to see.

00:07:05.124 --> 00:07:09.028
But source, sample,
detector from the 60s-ish.

00:07:09.028 --> 00:07:09.762
OK.

00:07:09.762 --> 00:07:11.364
[LAUGHING]

00:07:11.364 --> 00:07:12.365
All right.

00:07:12.365 --> 00:07:14.000
So now it's easier.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:15.401
Of course, you can understand.

00:07:15.401 --> 00:07:17.570
I mean, some of these
things have different ways

00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:17.804
of doing it.

00:07:17.804 --> 00:07:20.139
But you can understand changing
the angle is going to be easier

00:07:20.139 --> 00:07:21.040
to move that sample.

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:21.674
Right?

00:07:21.674 --> 00:07:23.142
And then, maybe
the detector moves.

00:07:23.142 --> 00:07:26.312
But you might keep the
source x-rays fixed.

00:07:26.312 --> 00:07:27.580
And what happens?

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:30.582
Well, you do this, and
you generate a spectrum.

00:07:30.582 --> 00:07:32.784
So you're changing the angle.

00:07:32.784 --> 00:07:35.054
You're keeping this fixed.

00:07:35.054 --> 00:07:38.357
And you're saying,
what do I got?

00:07:38.357 --> 00:07:41.761
Well here, in this case,
I'm telling you what I got.

00:07:41.761 --> 00:07:43.429
I got aluminum.

00:07:43.429 --> 00:07:45.264
I put aluminum in there.

00:07:45.264 --> 00:07:48.668
And I shot x-rays.

00:07:48.668 --> 00:07:50.536
If anybody gives
you an XRD pattern,

00:07:50.536 --> 00:07:54.740
they got to tell you what
kind of x-rays they used.

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:55.241
Right?

00:07:55.241 --> 00:07:55.975
That's really important.

00:07:55.975 --> 00:07:57.376
At some point, you
got to know this.

00:07:57.376 --> 00:07:58.411
This is pretty important.

00:07:58.411 --> 00:08:00.446
The lambda is from
a copper target,

00:08:00.446 --> 00:08:03.249
and the k alpha lines
is 1.54 angstroms.

00:08:03.249 --> 00:08:04.283
And this is the spectrum.

00:08:04.283 --> 00:08:07.687
And I'm being asked to get the
crystal structure of aluminum

00:08:07.687 --> 00:08:08.955
and the lattice constant.

00:08:08.955 --> 00:08:10.556
And I can get that
because, look,

00:08:10.556 --> 00:08:12.458
I can simply read off peaks.

00:08:12.458 --> 00:08:14.193
And those are the
peaks, and I've kind of

00:08:14.193 --> 00:08:15.261
highlighted them there.

00:08:15.261 --> 00:08:15.862
2 theta.

00:08:19.131 --> 00:08:22.468
More for historical reasons,
you take 2 theta measurements,

00:08:22.468 --> 00:08:25.071
even though, in the Bragg
condition, it's just theta.

00:08:25.071 --> 00:08:27.073
OK, we can divide by 2.

00:08:27.073 --> 00:08:27.573
Right?

00:08:27.573 --> 00:08:29.408
And so here are
those first 4 peaks.

00:08:29.408 --> 00:08:30.710
And I've just listed them here.

00:08:30.710 --> 00:08:33.212
Peak 1, 2, 3, 4.

00:08:33.212 --> 00:08:35.847
Sin squared of half of that.

00:08:35.847 --> 00:08:36.782
Gesundheit.

00:08:36.782 --> 00:08:38.116
OK, there it is.

00:08:38.116 --> 00:08:40.019
And then, we have our recipe.

00:08:40.019 --> 00:08:44.457
I gave you this recipe as a
way to keep this constant.

00:08:44.457 --> 00:08:45.024
All right?

00:08:45.024 --> 00:08:49.662
It's an easier way to do it if
you scale and clear fractions.

00:08:49.662 --> 00:08:50.963
It's just easier.

00:08:50.963 --> 00:08:52.899
So that's why I've
given you that recipe.

00:08:52.899 --> 00:08:53.533
So let's do it.

00:08:53.533 --> 00:08:54.700
So first we normalize.

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:55.535
What does that mean?

00:08:55.535 --> 00:08:57.904
Well, you have the smallest
value of science course data.

00:08:57.904 --> 00:08:59.739
I'm just going to divide
everything by that.

00:08:59.739 --> 00:09:00.239
OK?

00:09:00.239 --> 00:09:01.440
So that's where we left off.

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:04.243
One because that's what
I'm normalizing by.

00:09:04.243 --> 00:09:07.547
And then, this
one would be 1.33.

00:09:07.547 --> 00:09:10.816
And this one would be 2.67.

00:09:10.816 --> 00:09:14.287
And this one would be 3.67.

00:09:14.287 --> 00:09:15.688
And so that's good.

00:09:15.688 --> 00:09:17.089
But I don't want fractions.

00:09:17.089 --> 00:09:18.691
It's going to be
easier for me to think

00:09:18.691 --> 00:09:20.893
about this as integers.

00:09:20.893 --> 00:09:22.328
And so we clear fractions.

00:09:22.328 --> 00:09:24.697
What does clear fractions mean?

00:09:24.697 --> 00:09:25.565
That!

00:09:25.565 --> 00:09:26.699
Clear the fractions.

00:09:26.699 --> 00:09:27.700
Get rid of them.

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:28.601
Make it integers.

00:09:28.601 --> 00:09:29.502
How do you do that?

00:09:29.502 --> 00:09:34.440
Find what you multiply this by
so that they're all integers.

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:35.575
OK, I got an idea.

00:09:35.575 --> 00:09:38.778
How about 3 times 3.

00:09:38.778 --> 00:09:41.981
In this case, times 3 equals 3.

00:09:41.981 --> 00:09:43.316
OK, good.

00:09:43.316 --> 00:09:46.285
And in this case,
well, I don't mean--

00:09:46.285 --> 00:09:50.289
OK, anyway, times 3 equals 4.

00:09:50.289 --> 00:09:52.124
Times 3.

00:09:52.124 --> 00:09:55.294
Don't multiply it by
a different number.

00:09:55.294 --> 00:09:57.263
I'm clearing the fractions
for the whole table.

00:09:57.263 --> 00:09:59.532
I'm multiplying by
the same number.

00:09:59.532 --> 00:10:00.533
OK, good.

00:10:00.533 --> 00:10:01.434
8.

00:10:01.434 --> 00:10:05.471
And this is times 3 equals 11.

00:10:05.471 --> 00:10:07.740
So I've got 3, 4, 8, 11.

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:10.943
And that's what I
need to work with.

00:10:10.943 --> 00:10:13.679
That's what I need to work
with because now, you see,

00:10:13.679 --> 00:10:14.680
it's now--

00:10:14.680 --> 00:10:17.683
I hope it's kind of starting
to make some sense--

00:10:17.683 --> 00:10:22.588
if I can get this
to be 8 squared

00:10:22.588 --> 00:10:27.593
plus k squared plus l squared,
well then I've done my job.

00:10:27.593 --> 00:10:29.195
I've kept this constant.

00:10:29.195 --> 00:10:29.996
That's the trick.

00:10:29.996 --> 00:10:30.496
Right?

00:10:30.496 --> 00:10:31.297
I've done my job.

00:10:31.297 --> 00:10:32.999
I've just made the math easy.

00:10:32.999 --> 00:10:35.368
If I can get h squared
plus k squared plus l

00:10:35.368 --> 00:10:39.238
squared to equal these things,
then I'm keeping it constant.

00:10:39.238 --> 00:10:39.805
So let's see.

00:10:39.805 --> 00:10:41.040
What would that be?

00:10:43.743 --> 00:10:47.680
So this has to be h squared--
let me just write here--

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:50.549
plus k squared plus l squared.

00:10:50.549 --> 00:10:52.952
OK?

00:10:52.952 --> 00:10:55.287
I want those to be
an h squared plus k.

00:10:55.287 --> 00:10:59.892
So if I do that, well, this
looks a whole lot like 1 1 1.

00:10:59.892 --> 00:11:00.893
That works.

00:11:00.893 --> 00:11:04.530
And for 4, 2 0 0.

00:11:04.530 --> 00:11:08.234
OK, and for 8, 2 2 0.

00:11:08.234 --> 00:11:13.739
And finally, for 11, 3 1 1.

00:11:13.739 --> 00:11:16.342
Aw, it's a beautiful thing.

00:11:16.342 --> 00:11:20.846
I've gone from angles to planes.

00:11:20.846 --> 00:11:22.181
These are planes.

00:11:22.181 --> 00:11:26.485
H, k and l mean one of these.

00:11:26.485 --> 00:11:28.020
It means one of those.

00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:31.791
That's what it means,
a set of those.

00:11:31.791 --> 00:11:38.330
