based on your
knowledge of crystals

00:24:05.143 --> 00:24:07.646
and now your knowledge of
x-rays and how x-rays interact

00:24:07.646 --> 00:24:11.750
with crystals you can do this.

00:24:11.750 --> 00:24:14.386
Oh, that went too far.

00:24:14.386 --> 00:24:20.392
OK, so now I'm going to get
to the why this matters.

00:24:20.392 --> 00:24:24.296
So there's two different
sources, two different targets.

00:24:24.296 --> 00:24:27.332
Remember, we can call
them sources or targets.

00:24:27.332 --> 00:24:34.306
And notice they both have
this continuous radiation.

00:24:34.306 --> 00:24:35.540
And I will talk about that.

00:24:35.540 --> 00:24:37.843
The [INAUDIBLE] will
end with a discussion

00:24:37.843 --> 00:24:40.745
a little bit on what
you can do with that.

00:24:40.745 --> 00:24:43.815
But for now, let's keep
focused on these lines,

00:24:43.815 --> 00:24:45.517
these discrete
characteristic lines.

00:24:45.517 --> 00:24:47.118
So there's the k
alpha from molybdenum.

00:24:47.118 --> 00:24:48.987
There's the k alpha for copper.

00:24:48.987 --> 00:24:57.963
OK, now Moseley, Henry Moseley,
was a brilliant scientist.

00:24:57.963 --> 00:25:02.267
And he was working with
Rutherford and others.

00:25:02.267 --> 00:25:08.006
And he was really interested
in looking at the trends.

00:25:08.006 --> 00:25:10.141
So he took all of
these elements.

00:25:10.141 --> 00:25:12.844
So from calcium all
the way to zinc.

00:25:12.844 --> 00:25:14.312
20 to 30.

00:25:14.312 --> 00:25:16.348
And he said I want
to look at the k

00:25:16.348 --> 00:25:18.917
alpha lines of all of these.

00:25:18.917 --> 00:25:21.620
I want to look at the K
alpha lines of all of these.

00:25:21.620 --> 00:25:25.757
And what he found
was extraordinary.

00:25:25.757 --> 00:25:26.791
He did more.

00:25:26.791 --> 00:25:28.126
38 in total.

00:25:28.126 --> 00:25:30.896
But I'm just going to show
you his data for these.

00:25:30.896 --> 00:25:31.630
OK?

00:25:31.630 --> 00:25:36.902
And what he did was absolutely
profound because what

00:25:36.902 --> 00:25:37.836
he noticed--

00:25:37.836 --> 00:25:38.703
there are the lines.

00:25:38.703 --> 00:25:40.171
These are actually
his measurements.

00:25:40.171 --> 00:25:40.672
OK?

00:25:40.672 --> 00:25:43.642
These are his k alpha lines.

00:25:43.642 --> 00:25:47.512
And what he noticed in
going from calcium down to--

00:25:47.512 --> 00:25:49.281
ohh-- brass?

00:25:49.281 --> 00:25:51.583
OK, we'll talk about
that in a minute.

00:25:51.583 --> 00:25:53.485
It's supposed to
be zinc, isn't it?

00:25:53.485 --> 00:25:54.586
Why is it zinc?

00:25:54.586 --> 00:25:55.687
Well, why is it not zinc?

00:25:55.687 --> 00:25:57.822
Let's think about that later.

00:25:57.822 --> 00:26:02.861
For now he noticed that this
has a square root relationship

00:26:02.861 --> 00:26:04.095
to the energy.

00:26:04.095 --> 00:26:06.998
So Moseley came up with--

00:26:06.998 --> 00:26:09.834
he was working on this in 1912.

00:26:09.834 --> 00:26:11.703
OK?

00:26:11.703 --> 00:26:15.373
You got to remember 1913.

00:26:15.373 --> 00:26:20.645
1912 was when Rutherford did
the gold foil experiments.

00:26:20.645 --> 00:26:24.149
It had been 44 years
since Mendeleev

00:26:24.149 --> 00:26:27.552
put his periodic table to
paper and published that.

00:26:27.552 --> 00:26:31.156
So for 44 years, we
had a periodic table.

00:26:31.156 --> 00:26:33.792
But see, the thing is,
there was a huge problem

00:26:33.792 --> 00:26:39.397
with the periodic
table because Mendeleev

00:26:39.397 --> 00:26:44.436
had this, sort of, brilliant
realization that periodicity--

00:26:44.436 --> 00:26:50.942
periodicity-- was related
to, both, the atomic mass--

00:26:50.942 --> 00:26:52.877
and remember, we
talked about this--

00:26:52.877 --> 00:26:57.115
and the properties, the
chemical properties.

00:26:57.115 --> 00:27:00.185
That chemical properties.

00:27:02.687 --> 00:27:07.926
That allowed him to create
in ordering of the elements.

00:27:07.926 --> 00:27:09.594
In ordering of the elements.

00:27:09.594 --> 00:27:11.429
That is still the
ordering, essentially,

00:27:11.429 --> 00:27:14.132
that we have today.

00:27:14.132 --> 00:27:18.970
But the problem is why did
they have that ordering?

00:27:18.970 --> 00:27:22.774
I didn't really tell you why,
sometimes, he was like, well,

00:27:22.774 --> 00:27:24.743
the properties win.

00:27:24.743 --> 00:27:26.277
OK, maybe the mass-- no, no.

00:27:26.277 --> 00:27:27.012
Properties win.

00:27:27.012 --> 00:27:29.280
Properties need to be
aligned in this column,

00:27:29.280 --> 00:27:30.782
so I'm I'm going
to move those over.

00:27:30.782 --> 00:27:33.351
Like, that's what he did.

00:27:33.351 --> 00:27:37.288
But he didn't know why, except
that it made sense to him.

00:27:37.288 --> 00:27:39.724
Moseley's experiments told us.

00:27:39.724 --> 00:27:40.959
They gave us the why.

00:27:40.959 --> 00:27:42.994
Aw, it was so important.

00:27:42.994 --> 00:27:45.630
And Moseley's law--

00:27:45.630 --> 00:27:49.734
Moseley's law--
was, essentially,

00:27:49.734 --> 00:27:52.270
him thinking about
the Bohr model

00:27:52.270 --> 00:27:54.606
for these characteristic x-rays.

00:27:54.606 --> 00:27:58.510
So he said that h nu--

00:27:58.510 --> 00:28:02.580
so that's the frequency
for some k alpha line--

00:28:02.580 --> 00:28:05.650
is equal to 13.6 ev.

00:28:05.650 --> 00:28:07.052
All that looks familiar.

00:28:07.052 --> 00:28:10.689
Times z minus 1 squared.

00:28:10.689 --> 00:28:14.359
And then, he did the
difference in energy,

00:28:14.359 --> 00:28:18.496
just like we've done now a
number of times in this class.

00:28:18.496 --> 00:28:19.764
That's what he did.

00:28:19.764 --> 00:28:22.233
And this is 3/4.

00:28:22.233 --> 00:28:23.034
Why this?

00:28:23.034 --> 00:28:24.903
Well, let's talk about
that in a second.

00:28:24.903 --> 00:28:37.348
So 13.6 ev times 3/4
times z minus 1 squared.

00:28:37.348 --> 00:28:38.917
Two things about this, right?

00:28:38.917 --> 00:28:45.590
One is why is it 1 over 1
squared minus 1 over 2 squared?

00:28:45.590 --> 00:28:49.227
Well that's because they
knew, or at least they

00:28:49.227 --> 00:28:52.464
were pretty confident, that
you had this positive charge

00:28:52.464 --> 00:28:53.631
in the middle here.

00:28:53.631 --> 00:28:56.034
That's the protons
and the nucleus.

00:28:56.034 --> 00:28:57.435
And then, you had
these electrons.

00:28:57.435 --> 00:28:57.936
Right?

00:28:57.936 --> 00:29:00.772
So you had, like,
the 1s electrons.

00:29:00.772 --> 00:29:03.908
And then, you had
another shell out here.

00:29:03.908 --> 00:29:06.211
And so they knew, OK, 2s.

00:29:06.211 --> 00:29:10.682
Maybe that's combined
with 2p, so it goes on.

00:29:10.682 --> 00:29:13.818
So what happens in
the x-ray experiment?

00:29:13.818 --> 00:29:16.387
Well what happens is you
shoot an electron in.

00:29:16.387 --> 00:29:19.357
Rank and just crank
the voltage up

00:29:19.357 --> 00:29:23.261
so high that an electron
could come and knock that out.

00:29:23.261 --> 00:29:23.762
Right?

00:29:23.762 --> 00:29:26.464
And so that's what did it.

00:29:26.464 --> 00:29:30.435
And so now one of these
can cascade down there

00:29:30.435 --> 00:29:35.507
and give off a k alpha photon.

00:29:35.507 --> 00:29:36.040
Right?

00:29:36.040 --> 00:29:37.609
This is nothing new.

00:29:37.609 --> 00:29:39.344
We've talked about this.

00:29:39.344 --> 00:29:42.180
But you see it here
in the equation.

00:29:42.180 --> 00:29:43.581
You see it in two ways.

00:29:43.581 --> 00:29:47.552
First, we're going from 2 to 1.

00:29:47.552 --> 00:29:49.521
1 squared minus 1/2 squared.

00:29:49.521 --> 00:29:50.955
3/4.

00:29:50.955 --> 00:29:51.723
Right?

00:29:51.723 --> 00:29:55.260
But second-- and
this was critical--

00:29:55.260 --> 00:29:56.628
the z minus.

00:29:56.628 --> 00:30:04.169
The z minus told us that
all these positive charges

00:30:04.169 --> 00:30:06.571
in here, all these
positive charges,

00:30:06.571 --> 00:30:09.874
are screened perfectly by
one electron, this one that

00:30:09.874 --> 00:30:11.342
was left.

00:30:11.342 --> 00:30:13.178
And it works.

00:30:13.178 --> 00:30:15.880
He assumed perfect [INAUDIBLE].

00:30:15.880 --> 00:30:17.782
So what do all these see?

00:30:17.782 --> 00:30:19.684
They see z minus 1.

00:30:19.684 --> 00:30:25.690
They see z minus 1 if z is
related to the atomic number.

00:30:25.690 --> 00:30:28.660
Now here's where
this was so powerful

00:30:28.660 --> 00:30:30.929
because when you-- and
if you can't read this,

00:30:30.929 --> 00:30:31.896
don't worry about that.

00:30:31.896 --> 00:30:35.433
I just want to show you that it
is a perfectly straight line.

00:30:35.433 --> 00:30:38.636
When you plot the K
alpha transitions,

00:30:38.636 --> 00:30:40.872
these are different elements.

00:30:40.872 --> 00:30:43.074
These are his
different elements.

00:30:43.074 --> 00:30:48.646
And if you plot the square
root of the frequency

00:30:48.646 --> 00:30:54.485
versus element, it is a
perfect line that holds.

00:30:54.485 --> 00:30:56.387
And so what Moseley
wrote in the paper

00:30:56.387 --> 00:31:00.491
is we have your proof in 1930
that there is, in the atom,

00:31:00.491 --> 00:31:01.793
a fundamental quantity.

00:31:01.793 --> 00:31:05.463
A fundamental quantity, which
increases by regular steps

00:31:05.463 --> 00:31:07.932
as one passes from one
element to the next.

00:31:07.932 --> 00:31:10.368
This quantity can
only be-- only be--

00:31:10.368 --> 00:31:13.238
the charge on the
central positive nucleus,

00:31:13.238 --> 00:31:15.607
of the existence of which we
already have definite proof.

00:31:15.607 --> 00:31:16.641
Now we know about that.

00:31:16.641 --> 00:31:20.278
We know from experiments
that Rutherford

00:31:20.278 --> 00:31:23.381
did that that nucleus
had the positive charge.

00:31:23.381 --> 00:31:25.884
But they didn't know
that it was connected

00:31:25.884 --> 00:31:27.852
to the position in
the periodic table.

00:31:30.655 --> 00:31:35.059
In fact, years later,
people talking about those

00:31:35.059 --> 00:31:35.593
experiences--

00:31:35.593 --> 00:31:38.830
I mean, people didn't even
take Rutherford's experiments

00:31:38.830 --> 00:31:44.135
as seriously until
Moseley's work came along.

00:31:44.135 --> 00:31:48.706
44 years had come
since Mendeleev.

00:31:48.706 --> 00:31:52.577
But Moseley gave it the
foundation that it needed.

00:31:52.577 --> 00:31:57.682
Periodicity is because
of atomic number.

00:31:57.682 --> 00:32:00.184
Periodicity is atomic number.

00:32:00.184 --> 00:32:03.288
That was not known.

00:32:03.288 --> 00:32:06.257
That z gives you
the periodicity.

00:32:06.257 --> 00:32:06.758
Right?

00:32:06.758 --> 00:32:09.460
And that gives you, also,
the number of protons.

00:32:11.663 --> 00:32:13.998
This was a time when they
didn't know what was going on.

00:32:13.998 --> 00:32:17.502
Why did the mass change so much
the neutron wasn't discovered

00:32:17.502 --> 00:32:21.239
till 1932, 20 years later?

00:32:21.239 --> 00:32:23.308
But this gave the
grounding that was

00:32:23.308 --> 00:32:25.843
needed to the chemistry
of the periodic table.

00:32:25.843 --> 00:32:28.146
It was a very
important discovery.

00:32:28.146 --> 00:32:29.480
Very, very important discovery.

00:32:29.480 --> 00:32:30.949
So that's my why this matters.

00:32:30.949 --> 00:32:38.456
And it's really tragic
because he died, tragically,

00:32:38.456 --> 00:32:41.292
in World War I at age 27.

00:32:41.292 --> 00:32:45.396
And the Nobel Prize
was not given in 1916

00:32:45.396 --> 00:32:46.798
for either physics or chemistry.

00:32:46.798 --> 00:32:48.232
He died in 1915.

00:32:48.232 --> 00:32:50.435
And most people, at the
time, believe that he

00:32:50.435 --> 00:32:53.071
would have won it at age 28.

00:32:53.071 --> 00:32:56.341
That's how important
that discovery was.

00:32:56.341 --> 00:32:58.209
OK, let's go back to brass.

00:32:58.209 --> 00:32:59.610
I got to go to brass.

00:32:59.610 --> 00:33:01.446
What's going on here?

00:33:01.446 --> 00:33:04.015
What is going on with brass?

00:33:04.015 --> 00:33:04.849
Right?

00:33:04.849 --> 00:33:08.219
Any ideas?

00:33:08.219 --> 00:33:11.956
Why didn't he just
put zinc there?

00:33:11.956 --> 00:33:14.325
It looked so good until brass!

00:33:14.325 --> 00:33:15.593
What is going on?

00:33:15.593 --> 00:33:19.664
Well, ha, here, I'll
give you a hint.

00:33:19.664 --> 00:33:21.799
OK, the melting temperature.

00:33:21.799 --> 00:33:22.734
Let's see.

00:33:22.734 --> 00:33:24.035
Ah, brass.

00:33:24.035 --> 00:33:35.046
OK, zinc melts
around 420 C. Brass,

00:33:35.046 --> 00:33:47.725
which equals zinc and copper,
melts at around 900 C or more.

00:33:47.725 --> 00:33:50.028
Greater than 900 C.

00:33:50.028 --> 00:33:53.231
That's a hint because if
we go back to the video--

00:33:53.231 --> 00:33:54.766
let's go back.

00:33:54.766 --> 00:33:57.268
Oh, the sound is on.

00:33:57.268 --> 00:33:58.436
Those are the electrons!

00:33:58.436 --> 00:33:59.837
A small portion of the time.

00:33:59.837 --> 00:34:03.508
Let me set that up because
I'm doing in the middle.

00:34:03.508 --> 00:34:05.676
The electrons are
coming off of a cathode

00:34:05.676 --> 00:34:07.779
because Rankin has cranked
the voltage way up,

00:34:07.779 --> 00:34:08.913
and pumped all the air out.

00:34:08.913 --> 00:34:10.281
So those electrons
are coming off

00:34:10.281 --> 00:34:12.817
with thousands and
thousands of KEV.

00:34:12.817 --> 00:34:13.351
OK?

00:34:13.351 --> 00:34:14.152
They're coming out.

00:34:14.152 --> 00:34:16.554
And that's what's
on the right here.

00:34:16.554 --> 00:34:17.288
There they are.

00:34:17.288 --> 00:34:19.123
The cathode is shaped
to focus the electrons

00:34:19.123 --> 00:34:21.125
onto a small portion
of the target, which

00:34:21.125 --> 00:34:25.463
increases the intensity
of the x-rays produced.

00:34:25.463 --> 00:34:27.065
The target is,
typically, a piece

00:34:27.065 --> 00:34:29.132
of tungsten or
molybdenum, which may

00:34:29.132 --> 00:34:32.603
be embedded in a stationary
water or oil coiled rod,

00:34:32.603 --> 00:34:34.806
or on a rotating disk.

00:34:34.806 --> 00:34:37.408
The high speed electrons
collide with the target

00:34:37.408 --> 00:34:40.011
and rapidly lose energy.

00:34:40.011 --> 00:34:41.946
There are two x-ray
producing interactions

00:34:41.946 --> 00:34:44.449
between the incident
electrons and the atom.

00:34:44.449 --> 00:34:45.149
Ah!

00:34:45.149 --> 00:34:47.118
Look at the power!

00:34:47.118 --> 00:34:50.855
You can feel the power coming
into that tiny little piece

00:34:50.855 --> 00:34:53.091
of metal.

00:34:53.091 --> 00:34:57.395
Now some of the time,
you get the transition

00:34:57.395 --> 00:34:58.596
that we talked about.

00:34:58.596 --> 00:35:02.900
You knock an electron out,
and you emit a photon--

00:35:02.900 --> 00:35:04.435
a k alpha photon--

00:35:04.435 --> 00:35:07.105
or maybe an l beta photon.

00:35:07.105 --> 00:35:08.439
Right?

00:35:08.439 --> 00:35:10.641
And sometimes you
slow the electron down

00:35:10.641 --> 00:35:12.677
so you get the Brahms fraulein.

00:35:12.677 --> 00:35:16.714
But there's so much energy
being pumped into this material.

00:35:16.714 --> 00:35:20.051
And a lot of times, those
electrons just collide and heat

00:35:20.051 --> 00:35:22.120
up the metal.

00:35:22.120 --> 00:35:25.423
That metal is getting
extremely hot.

00:35:25.423 --> 00:35:28.392
And in these experiments,
either you cooled it,

00:35:28.392 --> 00:35:32.930
or you might rotate it quickly
so that it gets to cool down.

00:35:32.930 --> 00:35:35.199
It gets a little break.

00:35:35.199 --> 00:35:37.135
Or you do both.

00:35:37.135 --> 00:35:38.970
Or you just throw up
your hands and you say,

00:35:38.970 --> 00:35:40.938
you know what, I'm just
going to go for an aloy

00:35:40.938 --> 00:35:45.276
that's got some zinc in it,
but won't melt every time.

00:35:45.276 --> 00:35:46.744
Yeah, that was a good idea.

00:35:46.744 --> 00:35:49.347
So you put brass in, OK, you're
going to get the copper lines.

00:35:49.347 --> 00:35:51.282
But you'll get the zinc lines.

00:35:51.282 --> 00:35:53.784
You'll get the zinc lines.

00:35:53.784 --> 00:35:56.254
And that was why he used brass.

00:35:56.254 --> 00:35:58.723
And that's why you can
see you get extra lines

00:35:58.723 --> 00:36:01.125
in the case of brass.

00:36:01.125 --> 00:36:02.059
Al right.

00:36:04.829 --> 00:36:05.830
Good!

00:36:05.830 --> 00:36:06.330
All right.

00:36:06.330 --> 00:36:12.236
Now I am very excited
about Moseley.

00:36:12.236 --> 00:36:16.174
You know, it allowed us to
actually understand this

00:36:16.174 --> 00:36:17.642
not just because.

00:36:17.642 --> 00:36:18.876
Look at this.

00:36:18.876 --> 00:36:22.313
Mendeleev was like, you
know what, cobalt and nickel

00:36:22.313 --> 00:36:23.748
can't be swapped.

00:36:23.748 --> 00:36:26.918
Hey, all you people who
just think about mass,

00:36:26.918 --> 00:36:29.120
you're not going to put
them in the order you think.

00:36:29.120 --> 00:36:31.722
I'm changing that.

00:36:31.722 --> 00:36:34.192
I'm changing it because
properties matter.

00:36:34.192 --> 00:36:36.594
Moseley said, no,
you're changing it

00:36:36.594 --> 00:36:41.265
because it's the right order
because of the blue numbers.

00:36:41.265 --> 00:36:44.168
You're changing it because
there is an actual count here

00:36:44.168 --> 00:36:45.169
that matters.

00:36:45.169 --> 00:36:48.506
And that is why your
periodic table is what it is.

00:36:48.506 --> 00:36:49.807
Absolutely critical.

00:36:49.807 --> 00:36:52.043
It put the lantinides
in the right place.

00:36:52.043 --> 00:36:55.846
It allowed the
prediction of elements--

00:36:55.846 --> 00:36:59.083
like [INAUDIBLE] and a number
of others that hadn't been

00:36:59.083 --> 00:37:01.085
discovered yet--

00:37:01.085 --> 00:37:04.689
and it set it all on this
much more solid grounding.

00:37:04.689 --> 00:37:07.124
So that is the
contribution of Moseley.

00:37:07.124 --> 00:37:08.693
I can't help it!

00:37:08.693 --> 00:37:11.963
I'm very excited so
I brought t-shirts.

00:37:11.963 --> 00:37:14.232
I knew I was talking
about Moseley.

00:37:14.232 --> 00:37:14.732
Now--

00:37:14.732 --> 00:37:15.333
Woo!

00:37:15.333 --> 00:37:16.901
I'm going.

00:37:16.901 --> 00:37:17.735
OK.

00:37:17.735 --> 00:37:18.336
All right.

00:37:18.336 --> 00:37:19.704
There.

00:37:19.704 --> 00:37:20.771
And in the middle.

00:37:20.771 --> 00:37:21.872
And over there.

00:37:21.872 --> 00:37:22.573
[CHEERING]

00:37:22.573 --> 00:37:23.507
And over there.

00:37:23.507 --> 00:37:24.308
[CHEERING]

00:37:24.308 --> 00:37:25.209
And over there.

00:37:25.209 --> 00:37:26.410
This way!

00:37:26.410 --> 00:37:27.878
And, oh!

00:37:27.878 --> 00:37:28.913
That's a bad arm.

00:37:28.913 --> 00:37:32.183
[CHEERING]

00:37:32.183 --> 00:37:33.684
And up there.

00:37:33.684 --> 00:37:35.286
[CHEERING]

00:37:35.286 --> 00:37:35.820
Hold on.

00:37:35.820 --> 00:37:36.654
Hold on now.

00:37:36.654 --> 00:37:37.688
OK, there.

00:37:37.688 --> 00:37:38.656
There.

00:37:38.656 --> 00:37:41.525
And this is all for Moseley.

00:37:41.525 --> 00:37:42.393
Ah!

00:37:42.393 --> 00:37:43.894
This is all for Mosley!

00:37:43.894 --> 00:37:45.763
[CHEERING]

00:37:48.566 --> 00:37:51.369
All right.

00:37:51.369 --> 00:37:54.138
Now, OK.

00:37:54.138 --> 00:37:56.073
[SIDE CONVERSATION]

00:37:59.443 --> 00:38:08.919
Now apart from me showing off--

00:38:08.919 --> 00:38:10.121
I don't have much of an arm--

00:38:10.121 --> 00:38:12.757
one thing I'd like to say is, if
you did already get a t-shirt,

00:38:12.757 --> 00:38:14.792
please, just hand it off
to a friend or somebody.

00:38:14.792 --> 00:38:15.793
You know, let's share.

00:38:15.793 --> 00:38:18.596
Sharing is always caring.

00:38:18.596 --> 00:38:22.366
Now here's another type of
x-ray I want to talk about.

00:38:22.366 --> 00:38:30.141
And z, actually, this
is much less used today.

00:38:30.141 --> 00:38:31.676
I hope you've gotten
a sense of how

00:38:31.676 --> 00:38:34.578
powerful these
characteristic x-rays are.

00:38:34.578 --> 00:38:37.081
You know exactly what
the wavelength is.

00:38:37.081 --> 00:38:39.717
But in the very earliest
XRD experiments,

00:38:39.717 --> 00:38:43.487
it was the Bremsstrahlung
radiation that was used.

00:38:43.487 --> 00:38:44.989
And that was by Van Laway.

00:38:44.989 --> 00:38:46.891
So I want to just
tell you about that.

00:38:46.891 --> 00:38:48.993
And so here's an
example of an Mo target.

00:38:48.993 --> 00:38:52.763
Now let's remind ourselves
what we're looking at.

00:38:52.763 --> 00:38:55.499
These are x-rays.

00:38:55.499 --> 00:38:59.170
These are x-ray intensities
versus wavelength.

00:38:59.170 --> 00:39:02.740
And this corresponds
as 5, 10, 15, 20.

00:39:02.740 --> 00:39:04.408
That corresponds
to the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:39:04.408 --> 00:39:04.909
Electrons.

00:39:04.909 --> 00:39:08.946
That's thousands of electron
volts of the incoming electron.

00:39:08.946 --> 00:39:13.317
That electron that came in
here and knocked this out

00:39:13.317 --> 00:39:14.852
from the cathode.

00:39:14.852 --> 00:39:15.486
Right?

00:39:15.486 --> 00:39:15.986
OK.

00:39:15.986 --> 00:39:17.822
Now that electron--
if it comes in,

00:39:17.822 --> 00:39:19.256
and we talked
about this already,

00:39:19.256 --> 00:39:22.026
but let me remind you-- if
it comes in at, kind of,

00:39:22.026 --> 00:39:23.627
a low energy, well,
you're still going

00:39:23.627 --> 00:39:27.698
to get this continuous spectrum.

00:39:27.698 --> 00:39:28.933
This continuous spectrum.

00:39:28.933 --> 00:39:29.433
Oh!

00:39:29.433 --> 00:39:31.035
Why does it look like that?

00:39:31.035 --> 00:39:33.237
Beyond the scope of
what we need to know.

00:39:33.237 --> 00:39:34.772
But in case you're
wondering, it has

00:39:34.772 --> 00:39:37.041
to do with very
complicated effects that

00:39:37.041 --> 00:39:42.113
happen when x-rays come
off and then are reabsorbed

00:39:42.113 --> 00:39:43.047
by the same material.

00:39:43.047 --> 00:39:44.548
You can imagine
that that could lead

00:39:44.548 --> 00:39:47.518
to all sorts of interesting
cascades of absorption

00:39:47.518 --> 00:39:48.285
and emission.

00:39:48.285 --> 00:39:49.620
That's what leads to this shape.

00:39:49.620 --> 00:39:52.590
But we don't need to
know those details.

00:39:52.590 --> 00:39:54.592
What we need to know
is that as you increase

00:39:54.592 --> 00:39:56.627
the power of those
incident electrons,

00:39:56.627 --> 00:40:00.231
you're going to get more and
more intensity of x-rays.

00:40:00.231 --> 00:40:03.067
And this goes down.

00:40:03.067 --> 00:40:05.936
The minimum wavelength
or maximum energy

00:40:05.936 --> 00:40:07.671
of the continuous spectrum--

00:40:07.671 --> 00:40:10.908
the maximum energy goes up
because the maximum energy

00:40:10.908 --> 00:40:12.476
you can get in the
continuous spectrum

00:40:12.476 --> 00:40:15.746
is equal to the incident
electron itself.

00:40:15.746 --> 00:40:16.981
So it's set.

00:40:16.981 --> 00:40:18.349
Right?

00:40:18.349 --> 00:40:20.651
Remember the Duane
something limit?

00:40:20.651 --> 00:40:21.752
Duane Hunt?

00:40:21.752 --> 00:40:25.556
OK, we talked about that
already, how to get that value.

00:40:25.556 --> 00:40:27.425
Yeah, but then you get
to this certain point!

00:40:27.425 --> 00:40:31.562
And look, all of a sudden, the
characteristic lines appear.

00:40:31.562 --> 00:40:34.031
And those are the K alpha
lines for molybdenum.

00:40:34.031 --> 00:40:34.865
Why all of a sudden?

00:40:34.865 --> 00:40:37.334
Well because you
got over the amount

00:40:37.334 --> 00:40:42.840
of energy needed to kick out
a 1s electron from molybdenum.

00:40:42.840 --> 00:40:44.141
That's exactly why.

00:40:44.141 --> 00:40:45.676
Before that, you
had enough energy

00:40:45.676 --> 00:40:47.778
to produce x-rays,
but not enough

00:40:47.778 --> 00:40:49.480
to kick out the 1s electron.

00:40:49.480 --> 00:40:51.048
It's kicking out
the 1s electron that

00:40:51.048 --> 00:40:53.017
gives you characteristic peaks.

00:40:53.017 --> 00:40:53.918
OK, good.

00:40:53.918 --> 00:40:55.286
This is all reminder
stuff, but I

00:40:55.286 --> 00:40:56.821
want to get back on that page.

00:40:56.821 --> 00:40:59.924
But see now, OK, this
continuous part is also used.

00:40:59.924 --> 00:41:03.928
And it was used, like
I said, by Von Laue.

00:41:03.928 --> 00:41:10.868
And Von Laue was, again, a
really, really smart cookie.

00:41:10.868 --> 00:41:15.840
And he used the Bragg
condition to prove

00:41:15.840 --> 00:41:19.543
that the x-rays and the space
light of structure of crystals

00:41:19.543 --> 00:41:23.581
can be interfered with each
other to make this diffraction.

00:41:23.581 --> 00:41:25.816
But what he did was different.

00:41:25.816 --> 00:41:28.385
OK, so the Nobel Prize was
given for this epoch making

00:41:28.385 --> 00:41:29.320
discovery.

00:41:29.320 --> 00:41:32.723
And I'm actually very happy that
the people that worked with him

00:41:32.723 --> 00:41:36.393
also shared the Nobel Prize.

00:41:36.393 --> 00:41:38.329
Walter and Paul.

00:41:38.329 --> 00:41:39.797
What they did was
different though.

00:41:39.797 --> 00:41:42.132
They took this
continuous spectrum.

00:41:42.132 --> 00:41:51.442
And instead of fixing the
angle and the lambda--

00:41:51.442 --> 00:41:53.210
I'm sorry, instead
of varying the angle

00:41:53.210 --> 00:41:56.247
but having a fixed lambda,
they did the opposite

00:41:56.247 --> 00:42:00.484
because the continuous
spectrum has all lambdas.

00:42:00.484 --> 00:42:01.252
Right?

00:42:01.252 --> 00:42:03.521
And so you can imagine
how this works.

00:42:03.521 --> 00:42:04.321
Right?

00:42:04.321 --> 00:42:05.623
You can imagine how this works.

00:42:05.623 --> 00:42:11.295
So in the Laue
version of XRD, you

00:42:11.295 --> 00:42:15.933
shoot the x-rays, which have
all the wavelengths in them,

00:42:15.933 --> 00:42:17.868
at a crystal that's fixed.

00:42:17.868 --> 00:42:19.570
I'm not changing the angle.

00:42:19.570 --> 00:42:20.538
OK?

00:42:20.538 --> 00:42:22.439
I'm not changing the angle.

00:42:22.439 --> 00:42:26.076
And what you get are
still diffraction peaks.

00:42:26.076 --> 00:42:28.979
You get spots.

00:42:28.979 --> 00:42:38.756
So In Laue XRD, you
have a single crystal.

00:42:38.756 --> 00:42:41.759
It's got to be the same
everywhere, in this case.

00:42:41.759 --> 00:42:43.961
You have a single crystal.

00:42:43.961 --> 00:42:46.230
Theta is fixed.

00:42:46.230 --> 00:42:48.198
You don't move it around.

00:42:48.198 --> 00:42:53.871
But lambda varies continuously.

00:42:57.308 --> 00:42:59.276
OK.

00:42:59.276 --> 00:43:03.581
And the pattern that you
get reflects the symmetry.

00:43:03.581 --> 00:43:09.820
Pattern reflects the
crystal symmetry.

00:43:13.857 --> 00:43:14.792
How is that possible?

00:43:14.792 --> 00:43:20.598
Well, you can actually see
this by thinking about the--

00:43:20.598 --> 00:43:21.732
do I have another picture?

00:43:21.732 --> 00:43:23.334
I just have his one.

00:43:23.334 --> 00:43:26.437
You can see this by thinking
about that model, which is

00:43:26.437 --> 00:43:29.473
somewhere out there in the sea.

00:43:29.473 --> 00:43:31.475
Where is the model?

00:43:31.475 --> 00:43:32.710
I don't see it.

00:43:32.710 --> 00:43:33.611
Ah!

00:43:33.611 --> 00:43:35.446
All the way in the
back waving around.

00:43:38.282 --> 00:43:39.683
Let's see if I can draw this.

00:43:39.683 --> 00:43:42.086
Let's see.

00:43:42.086 --> 00:43:43.187
Ah, I'm not going to try.

00:43:46.457 --> 00:43:49.793
If I shoot x-rays that
have a fixed lambda in,

00:43:49.793 --> 00:43:52.129
but I vary the angle, then
as we've been talking about,

00:43:52.129 --> 00:43:54.765
there's going to be
certain angles where you

00:43:54.765 --> 00:43:56.266
get constructive interference.

00:43:56.266 --> 00:43:58.802
Now if I keep the
thing fixed and I

00:43:58.802 --> 00:44:03.974
shoot all lambdas into it, well,
take a look at the crystal.

00:44:03.974 --> 00:44:06.744
Imagine that I'm holding
that structure right now.

00:44:06.744 --> 00:44:07.544
OK?

00:44:07.544 --> 00:44:11.115
Well I'm going to see
this set of planes here.

00:44:11.115 --> 00:44:12.583
So I'm to see this
set of planes.

00:44:12.583 --> 00:44:13.951
Maybe they're
[INAUDIBLE] planes.

00:44:13.951 --> 00:44:14.518
I don't know.

00:44:14.518 --> 00:44:15.052
Right?

00:44:15.052 --> 00:44:17.388
And I'm going to
bounce off of that

00:44:17.388 --> 00:44:19.423
and constructively
interfere, maybe,

00:44:19.423 --> 00:44:21.025
if I've got the right lambda.

00:44:21.025 --> 00:44:23.794
But see, if I'm looking at
the crystal and it's fixed,

00:44:23.794 --> 00:44:26.230
I'm going to see also
there's another set of planes

00:44:26.230 --> 00:44:26.730
like that.

00:44:26.730 --> 00:44:27.731
Do you see?

00:44:27.731 --> 00:44:29.400
There's another set
of planes like that.

00:44:29.400 --> 00:44:31.602
And if I'm a different
wavelength-- not the same one,

00:44:31.602 --> 00:44:33.370
but a different one--

00:44:33.370 --> 00:44:35.773
I might constructively
interfere with those.

00:44:35.773 --> 00:44:39.643
But you can see that the angle
that I make will be different.

00:44:39.643 --> 00:44:42.079
And so I'll put a spot
in a different place.

00:44:42.079 --> 00:44:42.579
You see?

00:44:45.449 --> 00:44:49.219
So I've got all the
wavelengths there.

00:44:49.219 --> 00:44:53.424
And as they constructively
interfere with different planes

00:44:53.424 --> 00:44:56.627
that they see, they
make different spots.

00:44:56.627 --> 00:44:58.395
They make different spots.

00:44:58.395 --> 00:45:00.297
And that's what those are.

00:45:00.297 --> 00:45:03.434
In those spots, it's more
complicated to get crystal

00:45:03.434 --> 00:45:06.537
structure from those spots.

00:45:06.537 --> 00:45:07.738
It's more complicated.

00:45:07.738 --> 00:45:12.643
You can get symmetry
fairly easily.

00:45:12.643 --> 00:45:15.345
But getting the structure
itself is a little bit more

00:45:15.345 --> 00:45:15.846
complicated.

00:45:15.846 --> 00:45:18.549
It can be tricky.

00:45:18.549 --> 00:45:21.452
And for that, another
reason most work today

00:45:21.452 --> 00:45:26.390
is done with
characteristic peaks, XRD.

00:45:26.390 --> 00:45:28.826
But this happened before.

00:45:28.826 --> 00:45:30.260
And so I wanted
to show it to you.

00:45:30.260 --> 00:45:32.663
And this resolved,
actually, in one

00:45:32.663 --> 00:45:34.131
of Laue's very famous papers.

00:45:34.131 --> 00:45:36.567
It resolved the structure
of zinc sulfide.

00:45:36.567 --> 00:45:38.235
It was very important work.

00:45:38.235 --> 00:45:39.737
And those are the spots.

00:45:39.737 --> 00:45:41.972
Look at that pattern there.

00:45:41.972 --> 00:45:45.008
See, the thing that you
could get is the symmetry.

00:45:45.008 --> 00:45:49.546
Oh, by the way, by the way, I'm
not going to test you on Laue.

00:45:49.546 --> 00:45:52.483
I'm only talking about
it for five minutes here.

00:45:52.483 --> 00:45:54.284
So in this class,
I want you to know

00:45:54.284 --> 00:45:57.755
about using
characteristic peaks,

00:45:57.755 --> 00:45:59.723
and all the things we've
been talking about.

00:45:59.723 --> 00:46:03.227
Laue is just on the
side for knowledge.

00:46:03.227 --> 00:46:05.062
We won't test on this.

00:46:05.062 --> 00:46:09.066
But I want to end by
telling you a story.

00:46:09.066 --> 00:46:11.635
This tells you something
about crystal symmetry.

00:46:11.635 --> 00:46:14.104
What is symmetry?

00:46:14.104 --> 00:46:17.441
Well it just means, if I
rotate it, is it the same?

00:46:17.441 --> 00:46:19.276
Or if I translate
it, is it the same?

00:46:19.276 --> 00:46:22.346
Give me just one minute
because this is actually

00:46:22.346 --> 00:46:24.481
a really interesting story.

00:46:24.481 --> 00:46:27.284
That's translational symmetry.

00:46:27.284 --> 00:46:29.753
We looked at this
picture already.

00:46:29.753 --> 00:46:31.121
There's no rotational symmetry.

00:46:31.121 --> 00:46:33.724
To get back to this picture, I'd
have to rotate the whole thing

00:46:33.724 --> 00:46:36.493
by 360 degrees.

00:46:36.493 --> 00:46:39.530
But see, there are
crystals that actually

00:46:39.530 --> 00:46:44.768
have only rotational symmetry
and no translational symmetry.

00:46:44.768 --> 00:46:46.937
And Dan Shechtman
was the one who

00:46:46.937 --> 00:46:49.206
discovered these in the 1980s.

00:46:49.206 --> 00:46:51.508
Nobody believed him.

00:46:51.508 --> 00:46:54.978
His advisor told him to
go back and read the book.

00:46:54.978 --> 00:46:57.114
And you know who wrote the book?

00:46:57.114 --> 00:46:58.549
Linus Pauling.

00:46:58.549 --> 00:47:01.285
Linus Pauling wrote the
book on chemical bonds.

00:47:01.285 --> 00:47:05.088
Literally, the book was
called The Chemical Bond.

00:47:05.088 --> 00:47:07.524
And Linus Pauling took the lead.

00:47:07.524 --> 00:47:09.293
He won two Nobel
prizes, not one.

00:47:09.293 --> 00:47:13.130
He took the lead in
attacking Shechtman.

00:47:13.130 --> 00:47:16.099
Shechtman had discovered
quasi crystals.

00:47:16.099 --> 00:47:18.735
Shechtman had discovered
quasi crystals, but nobody--

00:47:18.735 --> 00:47:20.003
he was fired his papers.

00:47:20.003 --> 00:47:21.205
His papers weren't published.

00:47:21.205 --> 00:47:23.340
And for 10 years,
nobody believed him.

00:47:23.340 --> 00:47:24.942
Linus Pauling said
famously, "There

00:47:24.942 --> 00:47:27.144
are no such things as
quasi crystals, only

00:47:27.144 --> 00:47:28.478
quasi scientists."

00:47:28.478 --> 00:47:30.113
That's how bad it was.

00:47:30.113 --> 00:47:31.915
Oh!

00:47:31.915 --> 00:47:33.984
But Shechtman had his day.

00:47:33.984 --> 00:47:36.386
He won the Nobel Prize.

00:47:36.386 --> 00:47:37.721
And he had his day.

00:47:37.721 --> 00:47:39.122
And he was proven right.

00:47:39.122 --> 00:47:42.326
And these quasi crystals
are absolutely fascinating.

00:47:42.326 --> 00:47:45.329
Rotational, but no
translational symmetry.

00:47:45.329 --> 00:47:46.763
Have a great weekend!

